    "result_text": "The full suite is green: **68 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors, BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `ownerSegment` derived field on the owner response.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`OwnerSegment.java`** (new helper) \u2014 computes `<TIER>_<AREA>`: `PREMIUM` when `membershipLevel >= 3` else `STANDARD`; `METRO` when the locality is a known region (`NSW`/`VIC`/`QLD`) else `REGIONAL`. This follows the same style as the existing `OwnerTimezone`/`AgeBand` derivation helpers.\n\n2. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 added a `@Mapping` for `ownerSegment` that feeds `owner.getMembershipLevel()` and the mapper's existing `deriveLocality(owner)` (the same region source used for `locality`/`timezone`) into `OwnerSegment.of(...)`.\n\n3. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the read-only `ownerSegment` property to the `OwnerDto` schema so the generated DTO carries the field.\n\nThe two new acceptance tests (`Cp55Tests` \u2014 `STANDARD_METRO` for a known city, `STANDARD_REGIONAL` for an unknown city) pass, and nothing else regressed.\n\nOne note on the AREA rule: it keys off the derived *locality* (region), which prefers the postcode range and falls back to the city-to-region table \u2014 consistent with how the spec phrases \"the locality is a known region.\" An owner with a postcode outside the known ranges and an unknown city yields `UNKNOWN` \u2192 `REGIONAL`, matching the test's random-city case.",
